WEBVTT

00:00:00.104 --> 00:00:04.453
Every year, some children in the Valle de
Aburrá are diagnosed with type 1

00:00:04.466 --> 00:00:06.771
diabetes. Counting them sounds simple.

00:00:07.214 --> 00:00:10.000
But no hospital or clinic
sees every child,

00:00:10.417 --> 00:00:13.555
and some children are never written down
on the lists we can read.

00:00:13.997 --> 00:00:16.107
So how do you count the cases
nobody recorded?

00:00:17.242 --> 00:00:18.896
Think of a party with two doors.

00:00:19.326 --> 00:00:22.203
At each door, someone writes down
the guests who come in.

00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:24.286
Some guests are on the first list,

00:00:24.521 --> 00:00:26.695
some on the second, and some are on both.

00:00:27.125 --> 00:00:28.701
And a few slip in through a window,

00:00:29.065 --> 00:00:30.380
so they are on neither list.

00:00:31.472 --> 00:00:35.483
The overlap is the clue. Say list A
has one hundred children,

00:00:35.873 --> 00:00:38.764
list B has eighty, and forty are on both.

00:00:39.194 --> 00:00:41.655
List B found forty of A's
hundred children:

00:00:42.032 --> 00:00:45.951
four in ten. If B finds about four
in ten children overall,

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:49.220
its eighty children are about four
tenths of everyone.

00:00:49.662 --> 00:00:51.303
That makes about two hundred children,

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.011
so around sixty were on no list at all.

00:00:55.125 --> 00:00:58.654
This idea has a name: the
Lincoln–Petersen estimator.

00:00:59.097 --> 00:01:01.531
The total is list A times list B,

00:01:01.857 --> 00:01:06.453
divided by the number on both. Chapman's
version adds one to each count,

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.940
which behaves better when the
numbers are small.

00:01:10.053 --> 00:01:12.540
But it only works if the two
lists are independent.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:15.418
If one hospital refers
children to the other,

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.113
the same children turn up on both lists.

00:01:18.543 --> 00:01:20.535
The overlap looks bigger than it should,

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:22.918
and we would count too few
hidden children.

00:01:24.008 --> 00:01:26.573
That is why this study uses three lists:

00:01:26.847 --> 00:01:29.073
referral hospitals, the public network,

00:01:29.295 --> 00:01:31.821
and private clinics. With three lists,

00:01:32.042 --> 00:01:34.555
every child has a pattern: on A only,

00:01:34.815 --> 00:01:37.901
on A and B, on all three, and so on.

00:01:38.344 --> 00:01:40.154
Seven of these patterns can be counted.

00:01:40.584 --> 00:01:42.667
The eighth, children on no list,

00:01:42.888 --> 00:01:45.206
is hidden. Models use the other seven,

00:01:45.506 --> 00:01:47.381
and the way the lists depend
on each other,

00:01:47.641 --> 00:01:48.641
to estimate it.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.447
The answer is not one exact number,

00:01:51.825 --> 00:01:53.648
but an estimate with its uncertainty.

00:01:54.091 --> 00:01:58.322
Several models are averaged, each weighted by
how well it explains the data.

00:01:58.765 --> 00:02:02.333
That is capture-recapture: counting the
children nobody recorded.
